Let me understand the bug more clearly. The README says:

"The current-insert path in the shaping engine copies a glyph without ensuring the buffer index is valid, especially at end-of-text. This copies an not-initialized codepoint that is later used as a glyph id in a lookup, leading to a use-of-undefined-value observation."

Looking at the code in `hb-aat-layout-morx-table.hh`, around lines 636-668, the `transition` function handles `currentInsertIndex`:

```cpp
if (entry->data.currentInsertIndex !=0xFFFF)
{
    unsigned int count = (flags & CurrentInsertCount) >> 5;
    unsigned int start = entry->data.currentInsertIndex;
    const GlyphID *glyphs = &insertionAction[start];
    if (unlikely (!c->sanitizer.check_array (glyphs, count))) return false;

    bool before = flags & CurrentInsertBefore;

    unsigned int end = buffer->out_len;

    if (!before)
      buffer->copy_glyph ();// <-- copies glyph at buffer->idx
    /* TODO We ignore KashidaLike setting. */
    for (unsigned int i = 0; i < count; i++)
      buffer->output_glyph (glyphs[i]);
    if (!before)
      buffer->skip_glyph ();      // <-- advances idx

    buffer->move_to ((flags & DontAdvance) ? end : end + count);
}
```

The issue is at line 648(approximately): `buffer->copy_glyph()`. This copies `info[idx]` to `out_info[out_len]`. But if `buffer->idx >= buffer->len` (end-of-text), then `info[idx]` is past the end of the valid buffer — an uninitialized/invalid read.

Looking at the `drive` function in `hb-aat-layout-common.hh`:

```cpp
for (buffer->idx = 0;;)
{
    unsigned int klass = buffer->idx< buffer->len ?
        machine.get_class (info[buffer->idx].codepoint, num_glyphs) :
        (unsigned) StateTable<EntryData>::CLASS_END_OF_TEXT;
    const Entry<EntryData> *entry = machine.get_entryZ (state, klass);
    // ...
    if (unlikely (!c->transition (this, entry)))
        break;
    // ...
    if (buffer->idx == buffer->len)
        break;

    if (!last_was_dont_advance)
        buffer->next_glyph ();
}
```

So when `buffer->idx == buffer->len`, the klass is `CLASS_END_OF_TEXT`, and the transition is called with this end-of-text entry. In the `transition` function, if the end-of-text entry has a `currentInsertIndex != 0xFFFF`, it will try to `copy_glyph()` which does `out_info[out_len] = info[idx]` where `idx == len`, reading uninitialized memory.

The `copy_glyph` function at line 247:
```cpp
inline void copy_glyph (void)
{
    if (unlikely (!make_room_for (0, 1))) return;
    out_info[out_len] = info[idx];  // idx could be == len here!
    out_len++;
}
```

So the root cause is that `copy_glyph()` doesn't check if `idx < len` before reading `info[idx]`. This happens during the CurrentInsert path in `transition()` when `buffer->idx == buffer->len` (at end-of-text, but the `!before` branch calls `copy_glyph` without checking).

The use-of-undefined-value would be `info[buffer->idx].codepoint` where `buffer->idx == buffer->len`.

Now let me understand what format of input is expected. The harness accepts font files and uses the `hb-shape` tool to shape text. The input is a font file (likely TTF/OTF). For AAT morx table processing, we need an AAT font.

Let me look at what the harness expects more specifically.

